Verdict
-------
ratio = vol05_rms / vol10_rms = 0.030733398872818347 / 0.06146081118861979
      = 0.5000487022291207

Essentially exactly 0.5x (0.049% off), matching the "-6.0 dB / 50%" the
Properties panel showed at set-time. BUG32 (per-clip volume dropped by the
export mixdown) stays fixed under a fully manual, real-Chrome, real-export,
real-UI drive (import → place → select → set volume via the real textbox →
export via the real dialog → decode via the real Web Audio API). No crash,
no dropped audio, no A/V issue — this exercise (audio-only project) has no
video stream by design (see BUG17 / fidelity-matrix case 4, also verified
green in Task A).

Method note — why RMS was computed in-browser instead of via ffprobe
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The first attempt relayed the exported MP4's bytes out of the browser as a
base64 string through the chat/tool-call text channel and reconstructed it
with `base64 -d` on disk. That produced a truncated/corrupted file (8438 of
10610 expected bytes; ffmpeg's astats decode failed partway through — see
byte-length and checksum mismatch caught before trusting the result). Long
unstructured base64 strings are not a reliable channel for exact binary
transcription through this path. Switched to decoding and measuring RMS
directly inside the browser via the real Web Audio API
(`AudioContext.decodeAudioData` + manual sum-of-squares over the decoded
Float32 channel data) — this both avoids the relay entirely and exercises
a second REAL codec-decode path (Chrome's native AAC decoder) as an
independent cross-check on the exported file's validity, on top of the
ffprobe-based checks the automated fidelity matrix already does in Task A.

